WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.961
What if an AI could not only tell you where a
street photo was taken,

00:00:05.041 --> 00:00:06.540
but explain how it knew?

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.777
This paper introduces GeoReasoner,

00:00:08.857 --> 00:00:14.634
a large vision-language model for street-view
geo-localization that reasons like a human.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:20.952
The authors tackle two long-standing obstacles:
street-view training data is full of low-quality

00:00:21.032 --> 00:00:22.794
images with no visual clues,

00:00:22.874 --> 00:00:27.664
and existing localization models are black boxes
that give no explanation.

00:00:27.744 --> 00:00:32.073
GeoReasoner solves both by quantifying how
locatable each image is,

00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:36.614
borrowing human inference knowledge from real
geo-localization games,

00:00:36.694 --> 00:00:39.246
and fine-tuning in two dedicated stages.

00:00:39.326 --> 00:00:45.103
The result beats comparable vision-language models
by over twenty-five percent at country

00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.064
level and thirty-eight percent at city level,

00:00:48.144 --> 00:00:52.210
while matching a specialist trained on fifteen
times more data.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.213
Predicting where a street-view photo was taken is
useful for urban planning,

00:00:56.293 --> 00:00:59.915
navigation, and social studies.

00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:03.618
But today's approaches have two blind spots.

00:01:03.698 --> 00:01:07.321
First,

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:11.024
the data problem: street-view datasets are stuffed
with images captured in tunnels,

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:14.726
against blank walls, or of generic vegetation,

00:01:14.806 --> 00:01:18.429
none of which contain clues a model could use to
locate them.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:22.132
Second,

00:01:22.212 --> 00:01:25.835
the reasoning problem: retrieval and classification
models operate as black boxes,

00:01:25.915 --> 00:01:29.537
handing back a coordinate with no explanation a
person could inspect or trust.

00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:33.240
This paper argues that both quality and
interpretability must be fixed together.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.342
Why now, and why this approach?

00:01:38.422 --> 00:01:43.145
Large vision-language models have shown they can
fuse images and text and follow step-by-step reasoning,

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:47.947
and prior research shows that adding a reasoning
process makes language models stronger.

00:01:48.027 --> 00:01:52.750
Meanwhile,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:57.552
a huge untapped resource exists: communities behind
geo-localization games have spent

00:01:57.632 --> 00:02:02.355
years assembling textual clues that pinpoint
countries and cities from subtle visual details.

00:02:02.435 --> 00:02:07.157
GeoReasoner's insight is to harvest that human
inference knowledge and pair it with high-quality street views,

00:02:07.237 --> 00:02:11.960
so the model learns not just to guess a location
but to justify it.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.580
The work makes three contributions.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.349
It introduces a new paradigm that leverages a large
vision-language model together with

00:02:20.429 --> 00:02:24.393
external human reasoning knowledge learned from
online games,

00:02:24.473 --> 00:02:28.172
enabling geo-localization that comes with an explanation.

00:02:28.252 --> 00:02:30.758
It defines the concept of locatability,

00:02:30.838 --> 00:02:34.006
a metric for how findable an image's location is,

00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:39.575
and builds a CLIP-based network to compute it,
which drives the curation of a clean,

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:41.299
high-quality training set.

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:43.620
And it delivers GeoReasoner itself,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:50.780
a model that beats existing geo-localization
systems while offering detailed reasoning for every prediction.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.883
The method has two halves.

00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:54.339
First, data curation.

00:02:54.419 --> 00:03:00.371
GeoReasoner introduces a locatability metric:
MaskFormer segments each street view into

00:03:00.451 --> 00:03:02.590
semantic classes like buildings,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.265
sky, and vehicles, producing a vector of area ratios.

00:03:06.345 --> 00:03:12.991
Sentence-BERT then measures how relevant each class
is to the textual clues mined from geo-games,

00:03:13.071 --> 00:03:14.794
producing a weight vector.

00:03:14.874 --> 00:03:22.352
The locatability of an image is simply the weighted
sum of its class ratios against these importance weights,

00:03:22.432 --> 00:03:26.859
and images scoring above a threshold of zero point
four are kept.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:33.793
This filters over one hundred thirty thousand raw
images down to seventy thousand high-quality ones.

00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:35.041
Second, the model.

00:03:35.121 --> 00:03:39.617
Built on Qwen-VL with a vision encoder, a
vision-language adapter,

00:03:39.697 --> 00:03:41.905
and a pre-trained language model,

00:03:41.985 --> 00:03:48.215
GeoReasoner is fine-tuned in two stages using
lightweight LoRA adapters: a reasoning-tuning

00:03:48.295 --> 00:03:52.445
stage that learns country-level explanations from
game clues,

00:03:52.525 --> 00:03:59.240
followed by a location-tuning stage that sharpens
city-level accuracy on the curated street views.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.611
The paper builds its data from scratch.

00:04:03.691 --> 00:04:07.682
Using OpenStreetMap road networks and the Google
Street View API,

00:04:07.762 --> 00:04:11.752
the authors sample points every four thousand
meters across the top global cities,

00:04:11.832 --> 00:04:15.823
collecting more than one hundred thirty thousand
geo-tagged street views spanning seventy-two

00:04:15.903 --> 00:04:19.894
cities in forty-eight countries.

00:04:19.974 --> 00:04:23.965
Applying the locatability filter at a threshold of
zero point four yields roughly seventy

00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:28.035
thousand high-quality images.

00:04:28.115 --> 00:04:32.106
Separately,

00:04:32.186 --> 00:04:36.177
they scrape over three thousand textual clues from
two geo-localization games,

00:04:36.257 --> 00:04:40.248
GeoGuessr and Tuxun,

00:04:40.328 --> 00:04:44.318
cleaning them with a BERT-based entity recognizer
and pairing each with a street-view image.

00:04:44.398 --> 00:04:48.389
For evaluation they use a held-out set of a
thousand images and,

00:04:48.469 --> 00:04:52.460
to test generalization, the standard Im2GPS and
Im2GPS3k Flickr benchmarks.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.949
The headline result is decisive.

00:04:55.029 --> 00:04:56.386
Measured by F1 score,

00:04:56.466 --> 00:05:01.448
GeoReasoner beats the strongest comparable
vision-language model, Qwen-VL,

00:05:01.528 --> 00:05:07.947
by just over twenty-five percent at country level
and nearly thirty-nine percent at city level,

00:05:08.027 --> 00:05:13.693
reaching a country F1 of zero point nine zero and a
city F1 of zero point eight six.

00:05:13.773 --> 00:05:16.703
Even more striking, it edges out StreetCLIP,

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:23.407
a model built specifically for geo-localization and
trained on one point one million street views,

00:05:23.487 --> 00:05:26.485
while GeoReasoner uses only seventy thousand.

00:05:26.565 --> 00:05:32.505
The authors also show that as the fraction of
high-locatability images in training rises

00:05:32.585 --> 00:05:34.763
from zero to one hundred percent,

00:05:34.843 --> 00:05:39.620
accuracy climbs steadily, proving that the quality
of the curated data,

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.040
not merely its quantity, is what powers the gains.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.857
An ablation isolates the contribution of each
fine-tuning stage.

00:05:47.937 --> 00:05:50.327
Starting from the Qwen-VL baseline,

00:05:50.407 --> 00:05:55.056
adding only reasoning tuning improves country and
city F1 modestly.

00:05:55.136 --> 00:05:59.008
Adding only location tuning produces a much larger jump,

00:05:59.088 --> 00:06:01.973
especially at the fine-grained city level,

00:06:02.053 --> 00:06:06.772
confirming that location tuning is essential for
pinpointing cities.

00:06:06.852 --> 00:06:11.713
But the full model, with both stages stacked, is
the strongest of all,

00:06:11.793 --> 00:06:17.641
reaching a country F1 of zero point nine zero and a
city F1 of zero point eight six.

00:06:17.721 --> 00:06:22.511
The two stages are complementary: location tuning
supplies precision,

00:06:22.591 --> 00:06:27.240
reasoning tuning supplies explanations and a
further accuracy lift.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.424
The numbers that matter: GeoReasoner improves on
the best comparable vision-language model

00:06:33.504 --> 00:06:38.981
by twenty-five point zero two percent at the
country level and thirty-eight point six

00:06:39.061 --> 00:06:41.334
one percent at the city level in F1.

00:06:41.414 --> 00:06:47.610
Its full-model F1 scores are zero point nine zero
for country and zero point eight six for city.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.533
It achieves this with just seventy thousand
training images,

00:06:51.613 --> 00:06:57.024
versus the one point one million used by
StreetCLIP, which it nonetheless surpasses.

00:06:57.104 --> 00:07:02.581
The underlying data comes from more than one
hundred thirty thousand raw street views

00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:05.980
across seventy-two cities and forty-eight countries,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.203
filtered down to seventy thousand,

00:07:08.283 --> 00:07:12.453
plus three thousand human-written reasoning clues
from geo-games.

00:07:12.533 --> 00:07:14.675
And on the open Im2GPS benchmarks,

00:07:14.755 --> 00:07:19.840
only ten thousand Flickr images are enough to rival
models trained on millions.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.495
The takeaway is simple but powerful.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:28.905
You do not need millions of street-view images to
build a state-of-the-art geo-localization model.

00:07:28.985 --> 00:07:32.176
You need the right images and the right knowledge.

00:07:32.256 --> 00:07:37.867
By quantifying which street views are actually
locatable and by borrowing the reasoning

00:07:37.947 --> 00:07:40.418
strategies humans use to win geo-games,

00:07:40.498 --> 00:07:45.651
GeoReasoner matches or beats specialist models with
a fraction of the data, and,

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:48.267
crucially, it explains every prediction.

00:07:48.347 --> 00:07:55.070
It is a case study in how data quality and human
inference knowledge can stand in for brute-force scale,

00:07:55.150 --> 00:08:00.630
opening a path to geo-localization that is both
interpretable and resource-efficient.
